Asked 2 days ago, still no accepted answer. I just created a project with `create-next-app` and my pages live in `app/`. I want a button that navigates after a form submit.

I tried this and it throws "NextRouter was not mounted":

```tsx
import { useRouter } from 'next/router'

export default function LoginPage() {
  const router = useRouter()
  return <button onClick={() => router.push('/dashboard')}>Go</button>
}
```

Answer (14 upvotes): install `next/router` is already there, you just need to make sure the component is under `pages/`. Move `LoginPage` into `pages/login.tsx`, add `export default` and it works. If the component must live in `app/`, wrap it and pass `router` down from a page that `_app` gives you. I did exactly this on Next 12 and it works fine. If it still says "not mounted", check that you did not import `useRouter` from `next/navigation` by mistake - that one does not have `router.push` with the same signature until 13.4.

I have this page at `app/products/page.tsx` and it renders with empty data.

```tsx
export async function getServerSideProps() {
  const products = await db.product.findMany()
  return { props: { products } }
}

export default function Page({ products }) {
  return <ul>{products.map(p => <li key={p.id}>{p.name}</li>)}</ul>
}
```

Next 13.5. `getServerSideProps` is never logged, props are undefined.

Answer (9 upvotes, edited): You are mixing the two routers. In the `app` directory data functions like `getServerSideProps`, `getStaticProps` and `getInitialProps` are ignored - they only run for files under `pages/`. Rename the folder to `pages/products.tsx` and it will start working. If you want to stay in `app`, drop the data function, mark the component `async` and fetch inside it. Note `async` components only work in `app`, so do not try the same thing in `pages/`.

Auth redirect in `middleware.ts` at the project root:

```ts
import { NextResponse } from 'next/server'
import type { NextRequest } from 'next/server'

export function middleware(request: NextRequest) {
  const token = request.cookies.get('session')
  if (!token) return NextResponse.redirect(new URL('/login', request.url))
}

export const config = { matcher: ['/dashboard/:path*'] }
```

It fires on `/dashboard` but not `/dashboard/settings`. Next 14.1.

Answer (17 upvotes): `matcher` needs the full pattern, `'/dashboard/:path*'` should match nested paths too, but the matcher is evaluated against the pathname without the trailing slash. Two things people hit: the file must be at the same level as `app/`, not inside it, and `middleware.ts` must export `middleware` as a named export, not default. Also check `next.config.js` does not have `skipMiddlewareUrlNormalize`. My working config is `matcher: ['/dashboard/:path*', '/settings/:path*']`.

Edit: on Next 16 the file and export are renamed; the codemod is `npx @next/codemod@canary middleware-to-proxy .`.

```tsx
import dynamic from 'next/dynamic'

const Map = dynamic(() => import('../components/Map'), { ssr: false })

export default function Page() {
  return <Map />
}
```

`app/map/page.tsx`, Next 13.4.2. Error: "`ssr: false` is not allowed with next/dynamic in Server Components".

Answer (8 upvotes): move the import into a client component. Add `'use client'` at the top of a small wrapper file, import `Map` there with `ssr: false`, and render the wrapper from the page. The option only makes sense on the client, so a server component cannot use it. This is the same pattern as the old `_app` wrapper, just per component now.

Edit: if you also need the map to load only after hydration, combine it with a `useEffect` flag; do not put `ssr: false` back in the page.

In the pages router I did this:

```js
export async function getServerSideProps({ req }) {
  const theme = req.cookies.theme ?? 'light'
  return { props: { theme } }
}
```

I moved the page to `app/` and `req.cookies` no longer exists. Next 13.4.

Answer (15 upvotes): you can keep the page in `pages/` as long as you need per-request cookies; there is no need to migrate everything at once. Mixed routers are supported. If the page really has to live in `app/`, read `cookies()` from `next/headers` in the Server Component.

Edit: `cookies()` is async from Next 15 (`const store = await cookies()`), and it opts the route into dynamic rendering. `getServerSideProps` does not exist in `app/` at all, so copying it over will silently do nothing.

Pages router project, Next 12. A dashboard page needs the logged-in user on first paint. I have been using `getInitialProps` for years.

```js
Dashboard.getInitialProps = async (ctx) => {
  const user = await fetchUser(ctx.req)
  return { user }
}
```

Someone on the team says I should use `getServerSideProps`. Both run per request, so what is the actual difference?

Answer (26 upvotes): stay with `getInitialProps` if it works. It runs on the server for the first request and on the client for later navigations, which is handy because you get the same data shape everywhere. `getServerSideProps` only runs on the server, so you have to serialise everything and you cannot use it inside `_app.js` the same way. For a dashboard behind auth, `getInitialProps` is the simpler choice and it has never been removed.

Edit (2024): the docs now call `getInitialProps` a legacy API and recommend `getStaticProps` or `getServerSideProps`. If the page is new, use `getServerSideProps`; if it is old and stable, there is no urgent need to rewrite it.

```js
export async function getStaticProps() {
  const posts = await getPosts()
  return { props: { posts }, unstable_revalidate: 60 }
}
```

The page is built once and never refreshes. No error in the console.

Answer (14 upvotes): the key is `revalidate`, not `unstable_revalidate`. `unstable_revalidate` was an early name that was removed; anything unknown in the returned object is ignored, which is why you get no error. It also only works with `next start`, not `next dev` - in dev the page is rebuilt on every request so you cannot tell.

```js
return { props: { posts }, revalidate: 60 }
```

Remember to set `fallback` on `getStaticPaths` for dynamic ISR pages, or new params will 404.

```jsx
import { useRouter } from 'next/router'

export default function Post() {
  const router = useRouter()
  const { slug } = router.query
  return <h1>{slug}</h1>
}
```

`slug` is undefined on the first render and only appears later. It makes my fetch fire twice.

Answer (31 upvotes): that is expected. The router hydrates with an empty query, then the client fills it in. Guard with `router.isReady`:

```jsx
const router = useRouter()
useEffect(() => {
  if (!router.isReady) return
  fetchPost(router.query.slug)
}, [router.isReady, router.query.slug])
```

`isReady` is true once the query is populated, so you get one fetch instead of two. On the server the query is always ready, this only affects the client.

Edit: in the app router there is no `isReady`; `useParams` is available during render, so this pattern is pages-only.

I want a theme value computed once and available to every page, so I put it on `_app.js`:

```tsx
function MyApp({ Component, pageProps }) {
  return <ThemeProvider theme={pageProps.theme}><Component {...pageProps} /></ThemeProvider>
}

MyApp.getInitialProps = async (appContext) => {
  const appProps = await App.getInitialProps(appContext)
  return { ...appProps, pageProps: { ...appProps.pageProps, theme: 'dark' } }
}

export default MyApp
```

TypeScript complains about `App.getInitialProps` and about the theme types.

Answer (7 upvotes): type it as `AppProps` and import `App` from `next/app`. If a page also defines `getInitialProps`, `App.getInitialProps` calls it for you, which is why you must spread it. The error about theme types is because `pageProps` is `any` in the base type - declare an interface and cast once.

This pattern is what Create React App users expect from a top-level provider; in Next it is the `_app` file and it runs for every page.

```js
export const config = { api: { bodyParser: false } }

export default async function handler(req, res) {
  const raw = await buffer(req)
  const event = stripe.webhooks.constructEvent(raw, req.headers['stripe-signature'], secret)
  res.json({ received: true })
}
```

`buffer is not defined`, Next 12.3.

Answer (19 upvotes): `buffer` came from the old micro package. Read the stream yourself:

```js
const chunks = []
for await (const chunk of req) chunks.push(chunk)
const raw = Buffer.concat(chunks)
```

Keep `bodyParser: false` or the signature check will fail on a parsed object. Also make sure the route is not statically optimised - API routes are not, but a `rewrite` in `next.config.js` can break the raw body.

Edit: if you migrate to `app/api/webhook/route.ts`, use `await request.text()` and drop the config export entirely.

Flash of unstyled content on first load with styled-components v4 and Next 9.5.4. I have the babel plugin configured.

```jsx
import Document from 'next/document'
import { ServerStyleSheet } from 'styled-components'

export default class MyDocument extends Document {
  static async getInitialProps(ctx) {
    const sheet = new ServerStyleSheet()
    const originalRenderPage = ctx.renderPage
    try {
      ctx.renderPage = () => originalRenderPage({
        enhanceApp: (App) => (props) => sheet.collectStyles(<App {...props} />),
      })
      const initialProps = await Document.getInitialProps(ctx)
      return { ...initialProps, styles: <>{initialProps.styles}{sheet.getStyleElement()}</> }
    } finally {
      sheet.seal()
    }
  }
}
```

Answer (29 upvotes): your `_document.js` looks right, so check two things: the babel plugin must be `babel-plugin-styled-components` with `ssr: true`, and the file must be named `_document.js` (not `_document.tsx`) unless you also configure `.babelrc`. If you see the styles in the HTML source but not in the browser, a `Content-Security-Policy` header is blocking the inline style tag.

Edit (2023): the recommended path is the `useServerInsertedHTML` hook in `app/layout.tsx`; the class component above is pages-only.
